`memory_order_relaxed` provides no ordering guarantees with respect to other memory operations. If a producer writes data then sets a flag with `relaxed` order, the data writes might be reordered *after* the flag store. A consumer reading the flag as `true` (also with `relaxed` order) might then read stale or uninitialized data, leading to a data race or incorrect program state.
```cpp
// Producer
data = 42; // write to non-atomic data
flag.store(true, std::memory_order_relaxed); // Problematic
// Consumer
while (!flag.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)); // Problematic
// Use data (might be stale)
```
